  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to employ a multi-phase optimization process that combines geometric intuition and targeted local search. Unlike the previous algorithm that primarily uses soft-max surrogates, this approach will:\n1. Start with a geometric initialization that aligns the function's humps with specific geometric motifs\n2. Perform directional optimization on the most critical shifts directly\n3. Implement a hybrid repair mechanism that maintains both feasibility and candidate quality\n4. Use tighter constraints and penalized objectives where beneficial\n\nThe key limitations addressed are:\n- Improved identification of worst-case shifts\n- More efficient use of the computational budget\n- Better handling of boundary conditions\n- More robust optimization by focusing on geometric harmony\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. The primary objective is to minimize C5 = max_k integral h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx over k\n2. The function must satisfy:\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n3. The function representation is n-dimensional samples over [0,2] with dx = 2/n\n4. The evaluation is done via cross-correlation with (1-h) scaled by dx\n5. We will use:\n   - Gaussian process to estimate worst-case shifts\n   - Directional optimization for high impact regions\n   - Optional geometric kernel embedding\n\nImplementation procedure:\nAll no-boxed helper functions will be defined but not included in the final implementation:\n\n1. Initialization and setup:\n   - Start with initial parent if available, but if not use geometric_guess(n)\n   - Compute initial worst shift k_max\n   - Estimate potential worst shifts using Gaussian process\n\n2. Phase 1: Geometric initialization and repair (n_parallel=2)\n   - Modify parent geometry (rotation/symmetrization)\n   - Solve trust-region problem with direction sensitivities\n   - Compute additional worst shifts\n\n3. Phase 2: Focused directional optimization (budget_s/3)\n   - For each user-shifted upper bound:\n     - Use gradient descent in the curvature-constrained direction\n     - Apply coordinate-wise projection\n     - Maintain at least 2 parallel runs\n\n4. Phase 3: Hybrid repair (budget_s/10)\n   - Repair/trim at critical boundaries\n   - Compute directional penalties for overlap minimization\n   - Update only regions contributing to detected overlaps\n\n5. Phase 4: Memetic improvement (budget_s/5)\n   - Simulated annealing focusing on k_max\n   - Combined gradient estimation with directional perturbations\n   - Local inversion where geometry suggests\n\n6. Phase 5: Final integration (budget_s/6)\n   - Combine all improvements\n   - Refine using low-boost coordinate descent\n   - Project to 0/1 space for final candidate\n\nOperation for each candidate:\n\n- Initial x0 derived from geometric construction\n- Principal gradients based on exact formulation:\n  dC/dh_i = dx * [ \u03a3_CORREl_{active} (indicator_{overlap at shift k_l involves x_i}) - H(x_i)/2 * \u03a3_overlap_contribution ]\n- New point generation:\n  x_proposals = x_best + gamma * X_dirl + perturbation\n- Feasibility check:\n  sum((h-0.5)/100)**2 penalized if shifted from geometric mean\n\nSearch choices:\n1. Geometric keywords:\n   - Rotation: shift the parent's geometric center\n   - Symmetrization: enforce reflection symmetry\n2. Optimization parameters:\n   - Max dimensionality = n = 560 if parent provided, else default\n   - Alpha: penalty between 1 and 50\n   - Beta: perturb intensity [0,2.0]\n3. Dimension variations:\n   - n \u2208 {200, 300, 400, 500}\n   - Each requires different length scaling\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness:\n- Time estimates:\n  ...",
